Description: Fix pixel-accessor integer overflow on large frames (CVE-2026-54240)
 A signed 32-bit integer overflow in libde265's pixel accessor functions
 (get_image_plane_at_pos variants in libde265/image.h) computes the pixel
 offset as ypos * stride using signed 32-bit arithmetic. For HEVC streams
 whose dimensions exceed sqrt(INT32_MAX) (~46341px) -- which still pass the
 existing <=65535 SPS validation -- the multiplication overflows to a negative
 value, causing pointer arithmetic to reference memory ~2GB before the image
 buffer start (out-of-bounds heap read and write).
 .
 This backports the CVE-2026-54240-relevant portions of upstream commit
 bdca87569b9c63c2a7054d90ae4462dbb78d159a (first released in 1.1.1), which
 widen the image stride type from int to ptrdiff_t across the stride members
 and pixel-accessor functions so the pixel-offset math is performed in 64
 bits. The same 32-bit-stride / absolute-coordinate pattern is widened in
 sao.cc, motion.cc, intrapred.h, sei.cc and image-io.cc. The SAO buffer-size
 allocation fix from the same upstream commit is kept in the sibling
 CVE-2026-54241 patch to avoid double-applying that hunk.
 pic_width/height_in_luma_samples become uint16_t with static_asserts
 enforcing the size assumptions. The public C API keeps int for ABI
 compatibility (stride is bounded to <= ~65552 at the API boundary).
 .
 Backport note: reconciled by hand against 1.0.11 source. The sei.cc edit is
 applied to compute_checksum_8bit (renamed to compute_checksum only in a later
 unrelated upstream commit, not present in this version), and the upstream
 stride16 widening in that function is omitted because the 16-bit code path it
 touches does not exist in this version. sps.h keeps the existing #define form
 of MAX_PICTURE_WIDTH/HEIGHT.
Origin: upstream, https://github.com/strukturag/libde265/commit/bdca87569b9c63c2a7054d90ae4462dbb78d159a
Bug: https://github.com/strukturag/libde265/security/advisories/GHSA-ccfw-29x7-rrx3
Author: Dirk Farin <dirk.farin@gmail.com>
Last-Update: 2026-07-18

